    "ls|LS16_S8_U_210": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n (MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*210 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.05.2016 \n \nTRIBAL CARNIVAL \n*210. SHRI MANOJ TIWARI:  \n Will the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)   whetheradequate steps have been taken to preserve, protect and promote ethnic culture of \nthe tribals, if so, the details, thereof; \n(b)  the details of various Centrally Sponsored schemes under implementation for the purpose;  \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to organize National Tribal Carnival in the near future; \n(d)  if so, the details thereof along with the aims and objectives of organizing such carnivals; and \n(e) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government for showcasing and promotion of \nvarious facets of tribal culture on a large scale in the country? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS  \n(SHRI JUAL ORAM) \n \n(a) to (e) A Statementis laid on the Table of the House \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (e) of the LokSabha  Starred Question No. \n*210 asked by SHRI MANOJ TIWARI to be answered on 09.05.2016  \n(a) &  (b) :Under Central Sector  scheme of “ Grant-in Aid to Tribal  Research Institutes \n(TRIs)”, Ministry of Tribal Affairs  extends financial assistance to TRIs for protection and \npromotion of traditional culture and languages of Scheduled Tribes . Functions of  TRIs, inter -\nalia, include organizing tribal festivals  for promotion of tribal culture. Ministry has also been \norganizing tribal festivals viz. ‘Prakriti’ and ‘Vanaj’ in past years, to showcase the tribal culture. \nTribal guests also take partin Republic Day celebrations so as topromote tribal culture and spread \nawareness about the same. \n“Development of Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups ” (PVTGs) scheme aims  at \nplanning socio-economic development in a comprehensive manner while retaining culture and \nheritage of PVTGs.Further, Tribal Cooperative Marketing Development Federation of India Ltd. \n(TRIFED) organizes exhibitions like ‘Aadichitra’ and ‘Aadishilp’where tribals showcase their \nrich tribal heritage of art and crafts . TRIFED also participates in  OCTAVE – a dance festival of \nNorth Eastern Region.  \n \n(c) & (d):Yes, Madam. Tribal Carnival aims to show-case tribal ways of life including their \nculture, tradition and customs. U nderlying idea is to preserve and promote traditional culture \nthrough display of various facets of tribal life relating to culture, tradition, customs and skills. \nThis also aims to instill a sense of pride amongst tribals for their rich cultur e heritage.  Tribal \nartists and artisans get national level stage for exhibiting their talent. Thisalso facilitatesvisitorsto \nmake themselves aware about unique and diverse traditional culture of  tribal population in real \nperspective.  \n \n(e):Besides, Ministry of Culture has  also been releasing funds under Tribal Sub -Plan to various \ninstitutions of Central Government for protection and promotion of traditional culture and \nlanguages of Scheduled Tribes. The intervention includes activities of Zonal Cultu re Centres, \nAnthropological Survey of India (Kolkata), Indi ra Gandhi RashtriyaManavSangrahalaya \n(Bhopal), Indira Gandhi National Centre for the Arts, Central Institute of Buddhist Studies (Leh), \nLalit Kala Akademi etc. The State level Tribal Festival s are also specifically supported  for \nshowcasing and promotion of various facets of tribal culture. \n**** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_216": "1\n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.216\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 9.5.2016\n \n \nVAISAKHA 19, 1938 (SAKA)\n \n \n \nADARSH SMARAK YOJANA \n \n \n \n*216.\n \nSHRIMATI POONAM MAHAJAN: \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether t\nhe Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) proposes to implement a \nproject ‘Adarsh Smarak’; \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof including the number of monuments identified by the \nASI to implement the said project, State/UT\n-\nwise; \n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government is \nplanning to upgrade all the monuments managed \nby  ASI to the standards of the Adarsh Smarak in a phased manner; and \n \n(d)\n \nif so, the details thereof and the time by which these monuments are likely to \nbe upgraded?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE, CULTURE AND\n \nTOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) AND \nMINISTER OF STATE, CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n \n(a)to(d) \n \nA statement is laid on the table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n2\n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERR\nED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d\n) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 216 FOR 9\n.5.2016\n \n \n \n(a)\n \n‘Adarsh Smarak’ scheme has already been implemented.\n \n \n(b)\n \nThe details of 25 centrally protected monuments identified and included in \nthe list of ‘Adarsh Smarak’ in the country (State/UT\n-\nwise) is at Annexure. \n \n \n(c)&(d)\n \nNo, Madam.  Besides, b\nasic tourist amenities, Adarsh Monuments are \nprovided with additional facilities like Wi\n-\nFi, cafeteria, interpretation centre, \nbrail signage, modern toilets etc. However, these identified facilities would be \nmonument specific on the basis of actual require\nment and feasibility on case \nto case basis. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n3\n \n \n \n \n \nANNEXURE\n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 216 FOR 9.5.2016\n \n \nDETAILS OF PROTECTED MONUMENTS IDENTIFIED AND INCLUDED IN THE LIST \nOF ADARSH SMARA\nK IN VARIOUS STATES OF THE COUNTRY \n \n \nSL.NO.\n \nNAME OF MONUMENT\n \n \nSTATE\n \n1\n \nRang Ghar, Sibsagar\n \nAssam\n \n2\n \nVaishali\n-\n \nKolhua\n \nBihar\n \n3\n \nHumayun’s Tomb\n \n \nDelhi\n \n4\n \nQutb Complex\n \n5\n \nRed Fort\n \n6\n \nRani\n-\nki\n-\nVav, Patan\n \nGujarat\n \n7\n \nRock\n-\ncut Temples, Masroor\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n8\n \nLeh Palace\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n9\n \nMartand Temple\n \n10\n \nHampi, Bellary\n \nKarnataka\n \n11\n \nPattadakal Group of Monuments \n \n12\n \nElephanta Caves, Mumbai \n \nMaharashtra\n \n13\n \nDaultabad Fort\n \n14\n \nMandu \n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n15\n \nKhajuraho\n \n16\n \nKonark Temple\n \nOdisha\n \n17\n \nKumbhalgarh F\nort, Kelwada\n \nRajasthan\n \n18\n \nShore Temple Mahabalipuram\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n19\n \nBrihadeshwar Temple, Thanjavur\n \n20\n \nTaj Mahal, Agra\n \n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n21\n \nGroup of Monuments at Fatehpur Sikri, Agra\n \n22\n \nSravasti\n \n23\n \nSarnath, Varanasi\n \n24\n \nJageshwar Group of Temples\n \nUtta\nrakhand\n \n25\n \nHazardwari Palace, Murshidabad\n \nWest Bengal\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_217": "������������������� �\n��������������������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n�\n�������������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n�\n�������������������������� �\n�\n������������������������ �\n���������������������������������� �\n������������������������������ �\n������������������������������������� �\n�\n����� ���� ��������� ��� ������� ���� ����������� ��� �������� ���\n������ �\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������� �\n����������� ���� ����������� ���� �������� ����� ��� ���������� ��� �������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������ �\n������� ������� ����������� ��� ���������� ���� ����������� ��� ���� �����\n������������������������� �\n������������������������ ���������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������� �\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������ �\n�\n������� �\n������������������������������������������������ �\n����� �������������������� �\n�\n����� �������������� ��� ������ ������ ���� ������� ������� ��������� �����������\n������� ����� ��� ���������� ������� ��������� ��������� �������������\n��������� ������������ �������� ������ ������� ���� �������������\n��������� ��������� �������� ������ �������� �� �������� �������� �������\n�������������������������������� �\n��������� ��\n\n����������\n����� ������������� ����������� ��� ������ �������� ����� ������� ���������\n���������� ����� ���������� ��� �� ������������� ����� ������ ����� �����\n���������������� �������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ���� ��������� ��� ������� ��������������� �������� �������� ��������\n������������������������������������������� �\n��� ���������������������������������������������������� �\n������������������������������ �\n�����������������������